>
> That seems strange that the monster would attack you while fleeing.
> The only thing I can think of is if it were confused or stunned, it
> may have attacked on accident while fleeing.
>
> It's hard to know. IIRC a stethoscope will tell you their status (if
> you have one). If you use ranged weapons, you aren't a caitiff
> though, so if you really want to kill something you can throw stuff.
> Personally I don't worry about it; the penalty is -1 alignment each
> time, which is not all that much. Just try to avoid intentionally
> getting them running as you might with a Rogue.

There was nothing in the room which could cause confusion or stunning, no
one hurled or otherwise shattered a potion, and I had line-of-sight to the
monster the entire time, so I'm pretty sure I would've seen a message about
it getting confused/stunned.  And it seemed to rather deliberately chase
after me, rather than anything accidental.  (The only thing I can guess is
that there wasn't anywhere for it to run to when it first "turned to flee";
when I backed up, it "fled" into the newly empty space, noticed it was next
to me, and attacked.)

And I didn't have a stethoscope.  But why should you need a stethoscope to
tell if something is running away from you?  Seems like that ought to be
fairly easy to tell.  Ok, maybe not for spotted jellies.  :-)

The problem with the -1 alignment is that it hits you every time you hit the
monster; you could easily accumulate -5 alignment just killing one beastie
(I know I had to hit the one in question three times before it died), and
that can add up quickly.



